<p>German Chancellor Friedrich Merz said Friday in Würzburg that he would not currently advise his children, or other young Germans, to study or work in the United States, citing what he called a rapidly changing U.S. social climate and limited job opportunities even for highly educated people. Speaking at a Catholic gathering, Merz said Germany offered strong opportunities for young people and described himself as “a great admirer of America”. The remarks followed weeks of public friction between Berlin and Washington under President Trump over trade, the war in Ukraine and the Iran war. Merz said last month that the United States was being humiliated in the Iran war, drawing an angry response from Trump; days later, Washington announced a partial troop withdrawal from Germany and tariff hikes on European Union cars. Merz later wrote on X that he had “a good phone call” with President Trump and said the United States and Germany were strong partners in NATO. The New York Times reported that Merz initiated the call after the Würzburg comments.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/VgKvBD_gnHC6RJcEqhnU7o6-nW0PfRicIpHXxw8Pa5JIb71mJHQu2aKTmQyjgB5L7l6OK3qpap9dEZvoxBoaLlSYsSzSOiNGXhXf4CgvpMamX31uyeiZm8WYBykE0MMrrOIrLPxEFTBeQna_kD8XLsl7ybxqZKbaCLEvDsdKwuN5QcQxR6g2D-uM7x8' alt='Chancellor Friedrich Merz meeting with people in Würzburg, Germany, on Friday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Domestic message: Merz told the audience that people were too prone to think in “disaster mode” about the state of the world and urged a more optimistic tone.</li><li>Communication issue: Merz said he needed to explain his political course more clearly after recent criticism of his messaging.</li><li>Coalition tensions: Merz said the governing coalition may be arguing too much and delivering too little, while defending political dispute as part of democracy.</li><li>Event disruption: German reports said demonstrators interrupted parts of Merz’s Würzburg discussion with young people.</li></ul><blockquote>I would not recommend to my children today that they go to the U.S. to get an education and to work - Friedrich Merz</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Würzburg audience: Politico Europe reported that Merz’s statement about not recommending U.S. education and work drew applause, while his comment that his admiration for America was not increasing drew laughter. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/friedrich-merz-advises-against-us-study-work-social-climate/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>President Trump: Trump responded to Merz’s earlier Iran remarks by saying the German chancellor should focus on fixing his own “broken country.” (<a href='https://english.aaj.tv/news/330459033/germanys-merz-i-wouldnt-advise-my-children-to-live-in-us'>Aaj News</a>)</li><li>Friedrich Merz: In the domestic working-hours debate, Merz rejected the idea that the CDU viewed Germans as work-shy, saying neither he nor his party had called people in Germany lazy. (<a href='https://kurier.at/politik/ausland/friedrich-merz-usa-aufenthalt-abraten/403160007'>Kurier</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/germanys-merz-wouldnt-advise-his-children-to-live-in-us/article70983574.ece'>Germany&#x27;s Merz ‘wouldn&#x27;t advise’ his children to live in U.S.</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/123f2c88-459a-4991-afb6-bbaa0dbe97fd'>Germany’s Merz says he would not want his children to move to US</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/germany-news-chancellor-merz-says-he-wouldn-t-advise-his-kids-to-go-to-the-us/live-77168830'>Germany news: Chancellor Merz says he wouldn&#x27;t advise his kids to go to the US</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/15/world/europe/merz-trump-germany-us.html'>German Chancellor, After Iran Spat With Trump, Reports ‘Good’ Phone Call</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/friedrich-merz-advises-against-us-st